    <mj-text
      padding="10px 12px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        Bankers go home
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-image
          border-radius="10px"
          padding="10px 12px"
          width="600px"
          src="https://www.dubaisummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
          alt="REPLACE_ME"
          href="https://www.dubaisummary.com/"
          target="_blank"
        />

    <mj-text padding="10px 12px 16px 12px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Major international banks and professional services firms began evacuating offices in Dubai and elsewhere in the Gulf after Iran threatened to target US- and Israel-linked economic assets, including banks. Citi told staff to leave its DIFC and Oud Metha offices and work remotely, while Standard Chartered, HSBC, Deloitte, and PwC also <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/standard-chartered-evacuates-staff-offices-dubai-today-sources-say-2026-03-11/" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #102341; color: black;">took precautionary measure</a>s, including branch closures and work-from-home instructions. These moves followed escalating Iranian threats after reported strikes on an Iranian bank.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The disruption hits at the heart of Dubai’s identity as the Gulf’s safest and most reliable financial hub. DIFC had become home to hundreds of banks, hedge funds, wealth managers, and family offices, but this crisis is now testing business continuity, investor confidence, and the city’s safe-haven pitch. If tensions persist, firms may rethink regional staffing, office footprints, and even where they base operations.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Meanwhile:</strong> The broader security picture worsened. Drone debris reportedly hit near Dubai airport, injuring four people, while ships near the UAE and in the Strait of Hormuz were struck by projectiles. Gulf energy infrastructure and port operations have also been targeted, adding pressure to oil markets, shipping lanes, and the wider regional economy.</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
